        "original_id": "2b0108ae-f3a2-530f-a4e7-6e67a91c82af",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: M. S. Swaminathan, Norman Borlaug, Henry I. Miller, Gregory Conko\n- Concepts: Green Revolution in India, genetically modified food, high-yielding crop varieties\n- Products: wheat, alfalfa, clover, Onobrychis viciifolia\n- Awards: Nobel Peace Prize, Presidential Medal of Freedom, Congressional Gold Medal\n- Works: The Frankenfood Myth\n\nFacts:\n- During the Green Revolution, Onobrychis viciifolia was replaced by high-yielding alfalfa and clover species.\n- In the Green Revolution in India, a main development was higher-yielding varieties of wheat.\n- Higher-yielding wheat varieties were developed by many scientists, including Indian geneticist M. S. Swaminathan and American agronomist Norman Borlaug.\n- Norman Borlaug received multiple honors for his work, including the Nobel Peace Prize, the Presidential Medal of Freedom, and the Congressional Gold Medal.\n- The book The Frankenfood Myth argues against over-regulation of genetically modified food and features a foreword by Nobel Peace Prize-winner Norman Ernest Borlaug.",
